<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communist Party of Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PKI, Partai Komunis Indonesia, Communist Party of the Indies</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> (PKI) grew out of the Indies Social Democratic Association founded in 1914 and took the name Communist Party of the Indies in 1920, one of the first communist parties in Asia. It was banned after its failed uprisings of 1926–1927. Revived after independence, it became the largest communist party outside the communist states before it was destroyed in the mass killings of 1965–1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia.jpg" alt="Communist Party of Indonesia" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Historyandideology, vectorised by Zt-freak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_of_the_Communist_Party_of_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Western-style schools created a small educated elite, and the first modern organisations followed: Budi Utomo in 1908, Sarekat Islam, which grew into a mass movement in the 1910s, the <b>Communist Party of the Indies</b> in 1920 and Sukarno’s Indonesian Nationalist Party in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The period of parliamentary democracy under the provisional constitution of 1950 produced short-lived coalition cabinets and, in 1955, the first free general election, in which the Nationalist Party, two Islamic parties and the Communist Party (<b>PKI</b>) each won a large share.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Augusto Pinochet’s rule in Chile used authoritarian means to secure investment, bringing growth and high inequality, and Suharto’s extirpation of the <b>Communist Party of Indonesia</b> allowed capitalism to expand there.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
